{
  "dom_id": "questionContainer",
  "name": "Question 37",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14097559/assets/32fefe70890d179803a847fc597349948703176d1561a8c840e845fa7a06a872.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{y+2}{x-1}$\n- ${y}^{\u2032}=(x-1)(y-2)$\n- ${y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{y-2}{x}$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14097559/assets/a18f69bf5b2cb6b2984722f851e1eda017e48863517da7ee65597d7c25c46379.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- For $x\\ne 1,$ the slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $y=-2.$\n\n- For $y\\ne -2,$ the slopes of the slope field become vertical as $x\\to 1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I gives the required behavior. For $x\\ne 1,$ the right-hand side of ${y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{y+2}{x-1}$ evaluates to zero on $y=-2,$ and for $y\\ne -2,$\n\n$$\n|{y}^{\u2032}|=|\\frac{y+2}{x-1}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 1.\n$$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{\u2032}=(x-1)(y-2)$ does not evaluate to zero on $y=-2$ (the value depends on $x$).\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{y-2}{x}$ evaluates to zero on $y=-2,$ but \n\n$$\n|{y}^{\u2032}|=|\\frac{y-2}{x}|\\to |y-2|\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
